    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "On the graph, we see that $f(0)=0$, so $d=0$.\nWe also see that the function passes through $(1,1)$, so $f(1)=1$.\nPlugging in $(1,1)$ into the equation, we have $a+b+c=1$.\nWe must also have $f(-1)=-1$ since the function passes through $(-1,-1)$.\nSubstituting in $-1$, we get $a+b-c=-1$.\nWe now have a system of equations:\n\\begin{align*}\na+b+c&=1 \\\\\na+b-c&=-1 \\\\\n\\end{align*}\nAdding the two equations gives $2b=0$, so $b=0$.\nSubstituting back in, we have $\\boxed{a-c=1}$.\nWow, can you verify that $a=1$ and $c=-1$?",
