    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "[asy]\nimport graph; size(7cm); real lsf=0.5; pen dps=linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(10); defaultpen(dps); pen ds=black; real xmin=-3.25,xmax=4.25,ymin=-9.25,ymax=4.25;\n\npen cqcqcq=rgb(0.75,0.75,0.75);\n\n/*grid*/ pen gs=linewidth(0.7)+cqcqcq+linetype(\"2 2\"); real gx=1,gy=1;\nfor(real i=ceil(xmin/gx)*gx;i<=floor(xmax/gx)*gx;i+=gx) draw((i,ymin)--(i,ymax),gs); for(real i=ceil(ymin/gy)*gy;i<=floor(ymax/gy)*gy;i+=gy) draw((xmin,i)--(xmax,i),gs);\n\nLabel laxis; laxis.p=fontsize(10);\n\nxaxis(\"\",xmin,xmax,Ticks(laxis,Step=1.0,Size=2,NoZero),Arrows(6),above=true); yaxis(\"\",ymin,ymax,Ticks(laxis,Step=1.0,Size=2,NoZero),Arrows(6),above=true);\nreal f1(real x){return x*(x-1)*(x-2)/8;} draw(graph(f1,-3.25,4.25),linewidth(0.75));\nclip((xmin,ymin)--(xmin,ymax)--(xmax,ymax)--(xmax,ymin)--cycle);\n\nlabel(\"$(-2,0)$\",(-2,-6),SE*lsf);\nlabel(\"(0,0)\",(-0.1,-7),S*lsf);\nlabel(\"+\",(2,-8),NE*lsf);\n\n[/asy]\n\nThere are eight points associated with $a, b, c$ and $d$. The properties resulting from the intersections of these eight points are contained within the first equation.\n\nI will use (b) as an example to do the substitution to get an equation of a certain form.\n[(b-2)(d+4)+(5*d+6)-b*c-d-1]==-24.\nWe substitute the values into the linear form. \n2(0)+5(d+4)-3*(6+d)-d-1==24\n=> 5d+24-18-3d-d-1==24\n=>3d+5==24\n=>3d==19 =>\nd==6.33\n→2(0)+5(6.33+4)-3(two things)($d+.63$)-($d^.63^2-$5d)-($d^.63^3-6(6.63^2)+6.63$)==24\n8(0)+3(5*(6.63+4))-d-1.63(d-.63)+.63^3-6(d-.632)+6.63==24\n8(0)+15(10.63)-9.51d+option ???get it???=24.\n8a-4b+2c-d=7-6-9.51-6.63=-15.39(Answer)\nI hope that made sense. I am not good at type written equations but I hope it was understandable.\n\nAlso, like someone pointed out the solution with the same problem is welcoming for people to solve individually after they have learned the rules of constants and variables. \n\nEasily made.\n\nLast time I checked, we read x and y from left to right, so I would write d before c otherwise all your solutions are wrong and besides on the graph they get letters in place of $d, c, b, a$ I believe I read it so you can do so directly, i.e. $\\boxed 0$ instead of $\\boxed{-24}$ They are not all that different.\n\nThis isn't really true.  The terms come first in alphabetical order and then the rest are assigned sequential letters from the alphabet.  How you write it, its meaning is determined by where the letters you assign are in the alphabet, and within that group at the same spot.  The $x$ term comes with $d$, which is after $a$.  Why shouldn't that be so?\n\nAnother important reason:  if you use the correct marker, $d$ goes at a position in your term that has the highest value if you placed $d$ before $c$, no one will be able to sneer because there's a prompt saying \"Assign 1-6 Less than $d$\" if you don't agree.  It's pretty directly logical.  Other people can literally use the same letter for two different times and assign it any value between 1 and 6 without them confounding each other and sending confusing yet genuinely disturbing video messages.  Someone can tell in a second who the 2nd $d$ is and which one, if any?\n\nThat being said, $\\boxed{-24}$ doesn't make sense either (since it isn't equal to the prompt: $8a-3b-2c-d$, which I also suspect is covered in the pre-attempt walkthrough) unless I missed something, so the solution is $\\boxed{0}$. $0$ is technically equivalent to $-24$, which is also pretty clearly 0 by comparison.",
